// src/ipc_nfc.pdx -- libpdx-unicode-tables.M8 (paideia-os#2619, R131.M8)
//
// IpcNfc: IPC-boundary NFC hook. Enforces the canonical-form invariant
// that every string flowing across a paideia-os IPC boundary (sender ->
// receiver) is normalized to NFC on receive, so every downstream
// consumer sees a single canonical byte-sequence per logical string.
//
// =============================================================================
// SCOPE
// =============================================================================
//
// Two public entry points, both over the usual {bytes_ptr, bytes_len}
// surface the rest of this library uses:
//
//   _ipc_nfc_check(bytes_ptr, bytes_len)                  -> packed u64
//   _ipc_nfc_apply(bytes_ptr, bytes_len, out_ptr, out_cap) -> packed u64
//
// `_ipc_nfc_check` answers the fast question "does this byte range look
// like it is already in NFC?" without actually normalising. It is
// designed to short-circuit the hot IPC path for the common cases:
//
//   * ASCII-only payloads (every byte < 0x80): return is_nfc=1 immediately.
//   * Payloads containing precomposed Latin-1 Supplement / Latin
//     Extended-A code points but no combining marks (two-byte UTF-8
//     sequences whose lead byte is 0xC2..0xC3, 0xC4..0xC7, etc.): also
//     return is_nfc=1. A conservative fast heuristic treats these as
//     already-canonical since the combining-mark scan sees no 0xCC/0xCD
//     lead bytes.
//
// `_ipc_nfc_apply` is the normalisation forcing function: it calls the
// M3 `_norm_nfc` primitive directly, so a caller that gets is_nfc=0
// from the check (or that wants to normalize unconditionally) can call
// apply and be done. The output buffer is caller-supplied with the
// same OUT_TOO_SMALL / INVALID_UTF8 status semantics M3 uses.
//
// =============================================================================
// FAST-PATH HEURISTIC
// =============================================================================
//
// The check path is a single-pass byte scan, O(bytes_len), no table
// loads, no nested calls. Scan rule:
//
//     for each byte b in [bytes_ptr, bytes_ptr + bytes_len):
//         if b == 0xCC or b == 0xCD:
//             return is_nfc=0
//     return is_nfc=1
//
// The scan is a presence detector for UTF-8 2-byte sequences whose
// Unicode code point is in the Combining Diacritical Marks block
// (U+0300..U+036F -> UTF-8 lead bytes 0xCC and 0xCD). If no such lead
// byte appears, the payload CANNOT contain a trailing combining mark
// in that block, so the only non-NFC shape M3 could rewrite -- a
// base-plus-mark pair -- is absent. We can therefore declare the
// payload already-NFC without doing the (costly) decompose + sort +
// compose pass.
//
// This is a conservative over-approximation of the exact NFC predicate:
//
//   * It never reports is_nfc=1 on an input that is actually NOT in NFC.
//     The combining-marks block is the only Unicode block whose code
//     points are "mark"-category and whose precomposed partners live
//     in the M3 decomposition table (Latin-1 Supplement + Latin
//     Extended-A, which decompose to a base + a U+0300..036F mark).
//     Every entry in the M3 canonical table has a mark cp in
//     U+0300..036F.
//   * It MAY report is_nfc=0 on an input that is actually already in NFC
//     (e.g. a text containing a bare U+0300 combining acute with no base
//     before it, which M3 would leave untouched as its own grapheme-less
//     starter). That is a false-NEGATIVE on the check: the apply path
//     will re-emit the same bytes, so there is no correctness loss, only
//     the slow-path cost of a normalise call on bytes that would come
//     out unchanged.
//
// At later milestones (M3.1+ widens the CCC table, M3.2+ grows the
// decomposition table into Devanagari / Hebrew / Arabic / CJK blocks)
// the check must additionally scan for the UTF-8 lead bytes of those
// blocks' combining marks. The scan loop is designed so a later landing
// can grow it by adding more `cmp rax, 0xXX; je ...` lines without
// touching the control-flow skeleton.
//
// =============================================================================
// INTERACTION WITH M3 NORMALIZE
// =============================================================================
//
// `_ipc_nfc_apply` is a thin wrapper over `_norm_nfc`. Both take the
// same 4-arg (bytes_ptr, bytes_len, out_ptr, out_cap) signature and
// return the same packed (out_bytes << 32) | status shape. The wrapper
// exists for two reasons:
//
//   1. API clarity: callers at IPC boundaries name their call site by
//      intent ("I am normalising a payload crossing an IPC boundary"),
//      not by Normalisation Form choice. If a future milestone decides
//      the canonical IPC form should be NFKC instead of NFC (very
//      unlikely but not inconceivable; TR#15 does not mandate the
//      choice), the hook can be redirected at a single call site.
//
//   2. Future kernel integration: when a kernel-side IPC hook lands
//      (deferred; see "Kernel integration" below), its site will call
//      this entry point rather than M3 directly, so the kernel's
//      normalise obligation is captured in a dedicated symbol that
//      can be audited independently of the general M3 surface.
//
// The scratch buffer, status band, and reentrancy caveats are
// identical to M3's -- the IPC hook does not add its own scratch,
// does not introduce new error codes, and inherits M3's single-
// threaded ring-3 posture.
//
// =============================================================================
// KERNEL INTEGRATION (DEFERRED)
// =============================================================================
//
// The ideal shape would be a kernel-side per-syscall hook in
// `sys_ipc_recv` (and its specialised variants: sys_ipc_reply, the
// channel-specific receive paths like audio_routing_channel's recv,
// etc.) that normalises every string field in the incoming message
// transparently to the receiver. This is NOT landed at M8 and is
// unlikely to land kernel-side at any R131 milestone, for three
// reasons:
//
//   1. The normaliser is a ring-3 library (this file) that uses .bss
//      scratch and the full M3 decomp table (765 u32 = 3 KiB). Linking
//      a user-space library into the kernel's IPC fast path would mean
//      duplicating the table set and the scratch into kernel .bss and
//      calling the normaliser without a user-space stack. That is a
//      significant complexity cost for a feature whose correctness is
//      already achievable by the ring-3 convention of "every IPC
//      receiver calls `_ipc_nfc_apply` on string fields it will store
//      or forward".
//
//   2. The kernel does not know which fields of an IPC message are
//      "string" fields. The message layout is per-channel; each
//      channel's descriptor would need to carry string-field offsets
//      or a schema the kernel could walk. That extends the IPC ABI
//      surface considerably.
//
//   3. The receive-side ring-3 wrapper (which EVERY IPC channel has
//      today, since the kernel IPC primitives are capability-gated
//      and need userspace glue to extract and dispatch) is a natural
//      place to call `_ipc_nfc_apply` on each known string field.
//      The hook is thus most naturally a VPC (vfx-side protocol) /
//      pds / shell / terminal library convention rather than a kernel
//      syscall convention.
//
// A design-space ADR capturing the above (and the three alternatives
// that were rejected: kernel-side normalise, VDSO-side normalise,
// capability-env'd ring-3 normalise injected per IPC) lives at
// `design/toolchain/libpdx-unicode-ipc-nfc.md`.
//
// =============================================================================
// paideia-as ENCODER DISCIPLINE (ABSOLUTE)
// =============================================================================
//
//   - No `test rN, rN` -- every zero-check is `cmp reg, 0; je/jne`.
//   - No `and rN, imm64` -- no masks are needed in this file; the
//     scan compares bytes directly against imm8-safe constants
//     (0xCC and 0xCD) via `cmp reg, imm`.
//   - No 2-op `imul r, imm`; the scan uses a simple `add rcx, 1`
//     cursor advance.
//   - No multiline `pub let = "..."` -- single-line only.
//   - Module basename `IpcNfc` matches `ipc_nfc.pdx`.
//   - Labels prefixed `ipc_` (never `loop`/`if`/`let`/`fn` -- reserved).
//   - All `cmp reg, imm` immediates <= 0xCD < 0x80000000 so direct
//     `cmp reg, imm32` is sign-extension-safe. No MOVABS staging
//     needed anywhere in this file.
//   - All byte loads via `mov_b` (zero-extends into the full qword);
//     bytes are 0..0xFF so no sign-extension concern.
//   - Entry rsp%16==8 preserved per SysV. The check path is a pure
//     leaf (no nested CALL) so no callee-save push or stack-scratch
//     is required. The apply path makes one nested CALL into
//     `_norm_nfc` and uses a plain `sub rsp, 8` to realign.
//   - No `add reg, [mem]` anywhere.
//
// =============================================================================
// RETURN SHAPES
// =============================================================================
//
// `_ipc_nfc_check` -> packed u64:
//
//   bits [63:32]  is_nfc    (0 = heuristic says NOT already NFC;
//                            1 = heuristic says ALREADY NFC)
//   bits [31:0]   status    (0 = OK; the check path never fails
//                            because it does no UTF-8 validation --
//                            that is the apply path's job)
//
// `_ipc_nfc_apply` -> packed u64 (identical shape to M3 `_norm_nfc`):
//
//   bits [63:32]  out_bytes_written  (0 on failure; >0 on success)
//   bits [7:0]    status             (0=OK, 1=OUT_TOO_SMALL, 2=INVALID_UTF8)
//   bits [31:8]   reserved           (always 0; inherited from M3's shape)
//
// =============================================================================
// Upstream: paideia-os#2480 (R131 Unicode umbrella), paideia-os#2612
// (M1 UTF-8), paideia-os#2613 (M2 grapheme), paideia-os#2614 (M3
// normalize), paideia-os#2615 (M4 collation), paideia-os#2616 (M5
// width), paideia-os#2617 (M6 bidi), paideia-os#2618 (M7 locale),
// paideia-os#2619 (THIS M8 ticket). Design doc:
// design/toolchain/libpdx-unicode-ipc-nfc.md.

module IpcNfc = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1 -- STATUS CODES (returned in low 8 bits of _ipc_nfc_apply's rax)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // The three codes are the same values M3's Normalize module uses
  // (NORM_STATUS_OK=0, NORM_STATUS_OUT_TOO_SMALL=1,
  // NORM_STATUS_INVALID_UTF8=2). `_ipc_nfc_apply` is a direct pass-
  // through so its status byte IS the normalize status byte; this
  // module re-exports them for documentation value and so a caller
  // that only wants the IPC hook never needs to include the full
  // Normalize module.

  pub let IPC_NFC_STATUS_OK            : u64 = 0
  pub let IPC_NFC_STATUS_OUT_TOO_SMALL : u64 = 1
  pub let IPC_NFC_STATUS_INVALID_UTF8  : u64 = 2

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 2 -- is_nfc CONSTANTS (returned in bits [63:32] of _ipc_nfc_check)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Boolean-shaped: 0 means "the heuristic says the payload is NOT in
  // NFC" (i.e. apply should run); 1 means "the heuristic says the
  // payload is ALREADY in NFC" (i.e. apply can be skipped).

  pub let IPC_NFC_NOT_NFC : u64 = 0
  pub let IPC_NFC_IS_NFC  : u64 = 1

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 3 -- ERROR BAND 0xFFFFE780..0xFFFFE78F (M8, ipc-nfc)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Eighth 16-wide aligned user-space error-code slice taken by this
  // library (R131.M8, paideia-os#2619). Zero slots used at M8: both
  // public API points encode their status in the low 8 bits of the
  // packed return (check never fails; apply inherits M3's three-value
  // band fully fitted in 8 bits). The slice is reserved for later IPC
  // hook error kinds, if the hook grows a kernel-side companion whose
  // failure modes need a distinct band from M3's.
  //
  // Disjoint from every other user-space band in the paideia-os tree.
  // Grep confirmation at M8 landing time:
  //
  //   grep -rE '0xFFFFE78[0-9A-F]' paideia-os/{src,tools,design,tests}
  //     -> no hits outside caps.decl at landing.
  //
  // The internal band is a documented placeholder; no public constant
  // in this file exports it.

  // ==========================================================================
  // _ipc_nfc_check(bytes_ptr, bytes_len) -> packed_u64
  //
  //   rdi = bytes_ptr      (pointer to the first payload byte)
  //   rsi = bytes_len      (length in bytes; 0 is well-defined and
  //                         returns is_nfc=1 immediately)
  //   rax = (is_nfc << 32) | status
  //         status is always 0 (OK) at M8 -- the check does no UTF-8
  //         validation and so cannot fail.
  //
  // Fast-path heuristic (over-approximates "NFC"):
  //
  //   * Pure ASCII inputs (every byte < 0x80) fall through the scan
  //     without hitting 0xCC or 0xCD; return is_nfc=1.
  //   * Inputs containing precomposed Latin cps (two-byte UTF-8 whose
  //     lead is 0xC2..0xCB, 0xCE..0xDF) but no combining marks: also
  //     fall through; return is_nfc=1.
  //   * Inputs containing a byte equal to 0xCC or 0xCD anywhere:
  //     return is_nfc=0 immediately (the mark is present as the lead
  //     of a combining diacritical U+0300..U+036F sequence).
  //
  // Never a false-POSITIVE on NFC (every M3 decomposition's mark cp
  // lives in U+0300..U+036F, whose UTF-8 lead is exactly 0xCC/0xCD).
  // MAY be a false-NEGATIVE (reporting is_nfc=0 on an input that is
  // actually already NFC); false-negatives cost one extra apply call
  // that is a no-op on the bytes.
  //
  // Pure leaf. No callee-save push. All scratch is caller-save
  // (rax, rcx). Labels prefixed ipc_chk_.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _ipc_nfc_check : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (bytes_ptr: u64) (bytes_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-unicode-tables.M8 (#2619): _ipc_nfc_check scans the byte range for 0xCC or 0xCD lead bytes (UTF-8 encoding of U+0300..U+036F combining-diacriticals block, the only block whose code points partner with M3's precomposed table entries). Any hit returns is_nfc=0; no hit returns is_nfc=1. Status byte is always 0 (OK) -- the check does no UTF-8 validation. Pure leaf; no callee-save push; no stack frame; no nested CALL. All cmp immediates (0xCC, 0xCD) <= 0x80000000 so direct cmp-imm32 is sign-safe. Byte loads via mov_b. No test; no and-imm64; no imul-r-imm. Labels prefixed ipc_chk_.",
      block: {
        // rcx = i = 0 (byte cursor)
        xor rcx, rcx;

      ipc_chk_loop:
        // Loop termination: i >= bytes_len -> no mark seen.
        cmp rcx, rsi;
        jae ipc_chk_nfc;

        // Load the current byte.
        mov_b rax, [rdi + rcx];

        // Combining-marks block (U+0300..U+033F) UTF-8 lead byte:
        cmp rax, 0xCC;
        je  ipc_chk_not_nfc;

        // Combining-marks block (U+0340..U+037F) UTF-8 lead byte;
        // U+0370+ is outside the combining block but 0xCD still gates
        // conservatively (one false-negative-tolerated apply call on
        // Greek-letter inputs is cheaper than a second range check).
        cmp rax, 0xCD;
        je  ipc_chk_not_nfc;

        // Advance cursor and repeat.
        add rcx, 1;
        jmp ipc_chk_loop;

      ipc_chk_nfc:
        // No mark seen -> is_nfc=1, status=0.
        //   packed = (1 << 32) | 0
        mov rax, 1;
        shl rax, 32;
        ret;

      ipc_chk_not_nfc:
        // Mark seen -> is_nfc=0, status=0.
        //   packed = (0 << 32) | 0 = 0
        xor rax, rax;
        ret;
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // _ipc_nfc_apply(bytes_ptr, bytes_len, out_ptr, out_cap) -> packed_u64
  //
  //   rdi = bytes_ptr     (pointer to the first input byte)
  //   rsi = bytes_len     (length in bytes)
  //   rdx = out_ptr       (pointer to the output buffer)
  //   rcx = out_cap       (output buffer capacity, in bytes)
  //   rax = (out_bytes_written << 32) | status
  //         status in {0=OK, 1=OUT_TOO_SMALL, 2=INVALID_UTF8}
  //
  // Thin wrapper over M3's `_norm_nfc`. The ARG shape of _norm_nfc is
  // identical to this entry point's (both are 4-arg (bytes_ptr,
  // bytes_len, out_ptr, out_cap)), so NO arg-shuffle is required --
  // we just realign the stack and tail-call-like call.
  //
  // Entry rsp%16==8 (SysV). We `sub rsp, 8` to bring rsp%16==0 pre-
  // CALL, call `_norm_nfc`, then `add rsp, 8` and return the normalise
  // result unchanged.
  //
  // Labels prefixed ipc_apl_.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let _ipc_nfc_apply : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (bytes_ptr: u64) (bytes_len: u64) (out_ptr: u64) (out_cap: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-unicode-tables.M8 (#2619): _ipc_nfc_apply is a thin wrapper over M3's _norm_nfc with an identical 4-arg (bytes_ptr, bytes_len, out_ptr, out_cap) signature. No arg-shuffle needed -- rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx pass straight through. Entry rsp%16==8; sub rsp 8 for the nested CALL alignment; add rsp 8 and ret with the normalise result unchanged. No test; no and-imm64; no imul-r-imm; no byte load. Labels prefixed ipc_apl_.",
      block: {
        sub rsp, 8;
        call _norm_nfc;
        add rsp, 8;
        ret;
      }
    }

}
